 Top of the Morning 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Anthropic today filed for what could become the largest IPO of all time, ending months of "will they or won't they" speculation.

 Why it matters: This will be viewed as a referendum on the business of AI.

 The big picture: Anthropic's news comes on the same day that SpaceX secured $40 billion in debt financing to buy GPUs — with Elon Musk predicting mass neocloud death — and Meta saying it will seek up to $95 billion in equity financing for its AI buildout.

 All of this poses a capacity test for Wall Street, although each of the three offerings is targeting a different type of capital pool. If we assume Anthropic targets $100 billion — no actual target is listed on the S-1 — the three deals together represent around 0.73% of U.S. GDP. And that doesn't even include the $30 billion reportedly being raised in the private markets by OpenAI, which pushed its own IPO into 2027. One thing to watch going forward may be the volume of future debt financings, as companies don't want to risk losing their investment-grade ratings. By the numbers: Anthropic reported an $80.75 billion net loss on $16.29 billion in revenue for the first half of 2026.

 It didn't report Q3 numbers but, given that they've been leaked , don't be surprised if they emerge in an amended filing before pricing. Goldman Sachs and Morgan Stanley are lead underwriters, with 20 other banks listed. Zoom in: Risk factors identified by Anthropic include concerns "raised both within our organization and by other researchers, regulators, policymakers, and industry participants that sufficiently advanced AI systems could, if not developed, deployed, and operated with appropriate safeguards, oversight, and security measures, pose catastrophic or existential risks to humanity."

 It adds: "Our models are contributing to the pace of our own research and these risks will increase as AI systems substantially automate AI research and development if, for example, they accelerate AI progress beyond our ability to evaluate and safeguard new models." Cap table: Anthropic has raised over $126 billion in private funding, most recently in May at a $965 billion valuation .

 No outside investors were listed in the S-1, suggesting that none have at least a 5% ownership stake. Which is remarkable, considering that some firms have invested billions. The bottom line: Plenty of companies flip their S-1s and then get cold feet. Anthropic can't afford to be one of them, in terms of both its balance sheet and its reputation.

 
 
